  "body": " Easy Example  Diagonalizability diagonal matrices   Apply the to the matrix .    This diagonal matrix is in particular upper-triangular, so its eigenvalues are the diagonal entries . The standard coordinate vectors are eigenvalues of a diagonal matrix:  . Therefore, the diagonalization theorem says that , where the columns of are the standard coordinate vectors, and the is the diagonal matrix with entries : . This just tells us that is similar to itself.  Actually, the diagonalization theorem is not completely trivial even for diagonal matrices. If we put our eigenvalues in the order , then the corresponding eigenvectors are , so we also have that , where is the matrix with columns , and is the diagonal matrix with entries : . In particular, the matrices are similar to each other.   "
