  "body": " Geometry of a matrix with a complex eigenvalue  Complex eigenvalue matrices dynamics of   What does the matrix do geometrically?    First we find the (real and complex) eigenvalues of . We compute the characteristic polynomial using whatever method we like: . We search for a real root using the rational root theorem. The possible rational roots are ; we find , so that divides . Performing polynomial long division gives . The quadratic term has roots , so that the complete list of eigenvalues is , , and .  Now we compute some eigenvectors, starting with . We row reduce (probably with the aid of a computer): . The free variable is , and the parametric form is . For , we have . The free variable is , and the parametric form is . According to the , we have for .  The matrix is a combination of the rotation-scaling matrix from , and a diagonal matrix. More specifically, acts on the -coordinates by rotating counterclockwise by and scaling by , and it scales the -coordinate by . This means that points above the -plane spiral out away from the -axis and move up, and points below the -plane spiral out away from the -axis and move down.  The matrix does the same thing as , but with respect to the -coordinate system. That is, acts on the -plane by spiraling out, and acts on the -coordinate by scaling by a factor of . See the demo below.   The geometric action of and on . Click multiply to multiply the colored points by on the left and on the right. (We have scaled by so that the vectors and have roughly the same size.)     "
